PATCH 629: retire the Sound Blaster writer's original instructions (board:0026).

Original 132f..1339 is 50 ec eb00 0ac0 78f9 58 ee c3:
PUSH EAX; IN AL,DX; JMP; OR AL,AL; JS 1330; POP EAX; OUT DX,AL; RET.
Charge each original normal-core fetch at its I/O boundary through the shared
PIC clock. The signed status branch adds four instructions per busy poll;
it is not a fixed polling count or an aggregate measured elapsed delay.
Retain the complete saved DWORD EAX and emit its original low byte.

The complete original 534:536-ms trace reaches seven ready calls. A second
original capture sets only the first entry's device write_busy state to 7:
eight ff reads precede 7f, producing 40 instructions in that first call.
All fourteen complete calls preserve the original GP registers; original
lazy flags resolve to 3202. Both complete 600-ms captures retain the unprobed
39 frames, 27518 mixed PCM samples and endpoint. Code/hash/register/clock
evidence is in tools/oracle/sb_writer_132f_cases.json and sb-writer-132f/.

The reaching regression compiles the complete ordered-patched module with
actual port/PIC/DSP owners. The parent fails all 44 target phases, including
thin budgets, tick transitions and full-DWORD EAX. An independent endpoint
compiles original PIC/DSP/I/O source and replays every captured instruction;
its I/O timestamps and remaining budget must match the original trace first.

Scope is this leaf's byte I/O, fetch retirement and EAX result. Guest-stack,
caller flag transport, protected IRQ/IF, remaining startup and final mixed
PCM remain open. Generated source stays pristine.
--- a/fist_ext.c
+++ b/fist_ext.c
@@ -1857,11 +1857,16 @@
 {
   char cVar1;
   
+  fist_clock_charge_cpu_instructions(1); /* 132f PUSH EAX */
   do {
+    fist_clock_charge_cpu_instructions(1); /* 1330 IN AL,DX fetch */
     cVar1 = in(param_2);
+    fist_clock_charge_cpu_instructions(3); /* 1331 JMP, 1333 OR, 1335 JS */
   } while (cVar1 < '\0');
+  fist_clock_charge_cpu_instructions(2); /* 1337 POP EAX, 1338 OUT fetch */
   out(param_2,(char)param_1);
+  fist_clock_charge_cpu_instructions(1); /* 1339 RET */
   return param_1;
 }
 
